in › General\nBusiness & Economics / Economic History\nBusiness & Economics / Industries / General\nHistory / Europe / Great Britain / General\nHistory / Modern / General\nScience / Energy\nTechnology & Engineering / Power Resources / General--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCambridge University Press (https://assets.cambridge.org/97805217/66937/frontmatter/9780521766937_frontmatter.pdf)\nciteturn155search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; \nCambridge University Press\n978-0-521-76693-7 - Energy and the English Industrial Revolution\nE. A. Wrigley\nFrontmatter\nMore information\nEnergy and the English  \nIndustrial Revolution\nThe industrial revolution transformed the productive power of soci-\neties. It did so by vastly increasing individual productivity, thus deliv-\nering whole populations from poverty. In this new account by one of \nthe world’s acknowledged authorities the central issue is not simply how \nthe revolution began but still more why it did not quickly end. The an-\nswer lay in the use of a new source of energy. Pre-industrial societies had \naccess only to very limited energy supplies. As long as mechanical energy \ncame principally from human or animal muscle and heat energy from \nwood, the maximum attainable level of productivity was bound to be \nlow. Exploitation of a new source of energy in the form of coal provided \nan escape route from the constraints of an organic economy but also \nbrought novel dangers. Since this happened first in England, its experi-\nence has a special fascination, though other countries rapidly followed \nsuit.\nsir tony wrigley is a member and co-founder of the Cambridge \nGroup for the History of Population and Social Structure and a former \nPresident of the British Academy. His previous publications include \nPopulation and history (1969), People, cities and wealth (1987), \nContinuity, chance and change (1988), Poverty, progress, and popu-\nlation (2004), and, with R. S. Schofield, The population history of \nEngland 1541–1871 (1981).\n© in this web service Cambridge University Press\nwww.cambridge.org--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nE. A. WrigleyEnergy and the English Industrial Revolution. New York: Cambridge University Press. 2010. Pp. xiii, 272. Cloth $75.00, paper $30.99Reviews of BooksEurope: Early Modern and Modern | The American Historical Review | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/ahr/article-abstract/116/4/1197/45032)\nciteturn155search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\nJournal Article\n\n# E. A. Wrigley Energy and the English Industrial Revolution. New York: Cambridge University Press. 2010. Pp. xiii, 272. Cloth $75.00, paper $30.99\n\nGet access\n\nE. A.\n\n \n\nWrigley\n\n.\n\nEnergy and the English Industrial Revolution\n\n. New York: Cambridge University Press. 2010. Pp. xiii, 272. Cloth $75.00, paper $30.99.\n\nPat Hudson\n\nPat Hudson\n\nSearch for other works by this author on:\n\nOxford Academic\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\nThe American Historical Review, Volume 116, Issue 4, October 2011, Pages 1197–1198, https://doi.org/10.1086/ahr.116.4.1197\n\nPublished:\n\n01 October 2011\n\n\n## Extract\n\nThe central question of this work is not why and when the English industrial revolution started but why it did not stop. The gradual growth of the English economy from the sixteenth century was important, but only as a precursor to the radical break with the past that would occur sometime around 1800, when growth became self‐sustaining. All previous episodes of economic expansion in Britain and elsewhere had been followed by a corresponding period of retrenchment and decline caused by the pressure of population growth on available resources in the context of an economy limited by dependence upon organic (derived from plants or animals) sources of energy. The Pandora's box of sustained expansion, without reductions in living standards and the cut‐off of raised mortality, was opened with the shift to a different and more expansive source of energy: coal. The English economy was the first to become predominantly powered with fossil fuel and thus freed from the constraint of land scarcity, allowing population growth to continue and, over the long term, allowing the benefits of industrialization to percolate down and to improve the lot of the masses. The only other transformation comparable in magnitude, E. A. Wrigley argues, was the Neolithic food revolution. Economics, from Adam Smith through Thomas Malthus and the classical approaches of David Ricardo and John Stuart Mill, continued to be a dismal science because, even as the shift to exponential sustained growth was occurring, contemporaries still expected a ceiling to be reached.\n\nIssue Section:\n\nREVIEWS OF BOOKS > Europe: Early Modern and Modern\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nISBD view for: Energy and the English Industrial Revolution / › Colorado College Libraries catalog (https://catalog.coloradocollege.edu/cgi-bin/koha/opac-ISBDdetail.pl?biblionumber=455696)\nciteturn155search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; \n\n# Energy and the English Industrial Revolution /\n\nWrigley, E. A. 1931-\nEnergy and the English Industrial Revolution / E.A. Wrigley. - xiii, 272 pages : illustrations ; 24 cm\nIncludes bibliographical references and index.\nList of figures -- List of tables -- Acknowledgements -- Introduction -- Part I. A Sketch of the Argument. 1. The limits to growth in organic economies -- 2. The transition from an organic to an energy-rich economy -- Part II. Favourable Developments . 3. Agricultural change and urbanisation -- 4. Energy and transport -- 5. Occupational structure, aggregate income, and migration -- 6. Production and reproduction -- Part III. What Set England Apart from her Neighbours. 7. The timing and nature of change in the industrial revolution -- 8. Modernisation and the industrial revolution -- Part IV. Retrospective. 9. The industrial revolution and energy.\n\"The industrial revolution transformed the productive power of societies. It did so by vastly increasing the individual productivity, thus delivering whole populations from poverty. "
